          "prompt": "<p><strong>Câu II.1: sortDescendingByPrice() – 1.5 marks</strong></p><ul><li>File: Sorting.java</li><li>Method to Complete: <code>sortDescendingByPrice(Product[] a)</code></li><li>Task: sort a given product array in descending order of price, using an algorithm with the LOWEST POSSIBLE average-case time complexity.</li></ul>|||<p><strong>Câu II.1: sortDescendingByPrice() – 1.5 điểm</strong></p><ul><li>File: Sorting.java</li><li>Method cần hoàn thiện: <code>sortDescendingByPrice(Product[] a)</code></li><li>Nhiệm vụ: sắp xếp mảng sản phẩm GIẢM DẦN theo price, dùng thuật toán có độ phức tạp thời gian trung bình (average-case) THẤP NHẤT CÓ THỂ.</li></ul>",
          "starterCode": "// Product given here as already-implemented so this file compiles on its own.\nclass Product {\n    int code;\n    int price;\n    Product() {}\n    Product(int xCode, int xPrice) { code = xCode; price = xPrice; }\n    @Override public String toString() { return \"(\" + code + \",\" + price + \")\"; }\n}\n\npublic class Sorting {\n    // II.1 [1.5 pts] Sort a given product array in descending order of price,\n    // using an algorithm with the lowest possible average-case time complexity.\n    static void sortDescendingByPrice(Product[] a) {\n        //You should write here appropriate statements to complete this function.\n\n    }\n\n    // II.2 [0.5 pt] Briefly explain (in a comment, or in your written answer)\n    // why this sorting algorithm was chosen over the alternatives.\n}\n",
          "sampleSolution": "// Product given here as already-implemented so this file compiles on its own.\nclass Product {\n    int code;\n    int price;\n    Product() {}\n    Product(int xCode, int xPrice) { code = xCode; price = xPrice; }\n    @Override public String toString() { return \"(\" + code + \",\" + price + \")\"; }\n}\n\npublic class Sorting {\n    // II.1 [1.5 pts] Sort a given product array in descending order of price,\n    // using an algorithm with the lowest possible average-case time complexity.\n    // Quicksort is chosen: average-case O(n log n), the best average complexity\n    // achievable by a comparison-based sort (matching merge sort/heap sort's\n    // average case, but typically faster in practice due to smaller constants\n    // and in-place partitioning with no extra array allocation).\n    static void sortDescendingByPrice(Product[] a) {\n        quickSort(a, 0, a.length - 1);\n    }\n\n    private static void quickSort(Product[] a, int lo, int hi) {\n        if (lo >= hi) return;\n        int p = partition(a, lo, hi);\n        quickSort(a, lo, p - 1);\n        quickSort(a, p + 1, hi);\n    }\n\n    private static int partition(Product[] a, int lo, int hi) {\n        Product pivot = a[hi];\n        int i = lo - 1;\n        for (int j = lo; j < hi; j++) {\n            if (a[j].price > pivot.price) { // descending order\n                i++;\n                swap(a, i, j);\n            }\n        }\n        swap(a, i + 1, hi);\n        return i + 1;\n    }\n\n    private static void swap(Product[] a, int i, int j) {\n        Product tmp = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = tmp;\n    }\n\n    // II.2 [0.5 pt] Why quicksort:\n    // - Average-case time complexity O(n log n): the best average performance\n    //   among comparison-based sorts (shared with merge sort and heap sort).\n    // - In-place (O(log n) auxiliary stack space from recursion, vs O(n) extra\n    //   array for merge sort), and has smaller constant factors than merge\n    //   sort/heap sort in practice, since it works directly on the array with\n    //   simple index swaps rather than allocating/merging auxiliary arrays or\n    //   maintaining heap-order invariants.\n}\n",
          "expectedOutput": "(9,900) (8,800) (7,700) (6,600) (5,500) (4,400) (3,300) (2,200) (1,100)",
          "explanation": "<p>Verified by compiling and running Main. Quicksort (average-case O(n log n), the best average complexity achievable by a comparison-based sort) with the partition comparison flipped to descending order correctly sorts P — and since price = 100×code in this dataset, the result matches Câu I.2's descending-by-code order exactly.</p>|||<p>Đã kiểm bằng cách biên dịch và chạy Main. Quicksort (trung bình O(n log n), độ phức tạp trung bình tốt nhất có thể với sort dựa trên so sánh) với phép so sánh trong partition đảo ngược thành giảm dần sắp đúng P — và vì price = 100×code trong dữ liệu này, kết quả khớp chính xác thứ tự giảm dần theo code của Câu I.2.</p>",
